# WHAT THE WORKER MAY PUBLISH.
#
# THE REPOSITORY ROOT IS THE WEB ROOT. wrangler.jsonc points the assets
# directory at ".", so every file in this repository is a candidate for
# a public URL, including the ones nobody would ever write down as
# something to publish. Measured with wrangler's own walker: 842 paths,
# 773 of them inside .git, which is every commit ever made rather than
# merely the current files.
#
# SO THIS IS AN ALLOWLIST, NOT A DENYLIST, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
# A denylist has to name every mistake in advance and fails OPEN: a file
# nobody thought of is served. That is exactly how this went wrong. The
# first version of this file was a good denylist and it still missed two
# things, because neither had been invented when it was written:
# .jekyll-cache, left behind by a tool that no longer runs here, and
# CNAME, left behind by the host that no longer serves this.
#
# `/*` hides everything at the top level, and the lines under it put
# back the eleven things that are the site. It only matches one level
# deep, so re-including a directory re-includes what is in it, which is
# how the seven pages and the music work. Anything new is NOT SERVED
# until somebody adds it here on purpose, and publish-check.sh will say
# so, because it asks for all eight URLs and expects 200.
#
# .assetsignore itself does not need a line: wrangler drops it before
# uploading, confirmed in the deploy log as "Ignoring asset:
# .assetsignore". Everything else is here because it was watched being
# excluded rather than assumed to be.

/*

!/index.html
!/cabinet.js
!/cabinet.css
!/og-card.png
!/apple-touch-icon-180.png
!/sitemap.xml
!/robots.txt
!/music/

# The seven content pages, each a directory with an index.html in it.
!/car-games-online/
!/where-to-play-drift-hunters/
!/browser-games-official-sites/
!/drift-boss-one-button-games/
!/racing-games-online/
!/drift-games-online/
!/driving-games-online/
